urceSlug": "helu", "sourceName": "Helü", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "spring-and-autumn-period", "targetName": "Spring and Autumn Period", "context": "Helü's reign from 514 to 496 BCE was a significant phase toward the end of the tumultuous Spring and Autumn Period in ancient China."}], "places": [{"name": "Suzhou, China", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Ying (Jingzhou), China", "role": "Conquered Capital"}, {"name": "Wuxi, China", "role": "Historical Capital Region"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Helü", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "helü"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1279924", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hel%C3%BC_of_Wu"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Helü is remembered as a landmark person of the Classical era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries; Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati. Their legacy is studied through the lens of political thought and imperial studies.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Helü's reign dramatically altered the power dynamics in ancient China, leading to the temporary ascendancy of Wu and contributing to the decline of major states like Chu. His strategic innovations and military successes left a lasting impact on Chinese warfare and statecraft.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-09T09:19:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27196327549", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Helü or Helu was king of the state of Wu from 514 to 496 BC, toward the end of the Spring and Autumn period of ancient China. His given name was Guang ; he was initially known as Prince Guang.", "newValue": "Helü, born Guang, reigned as king of the state of Wu from 514 to 496 BCE, a pivotal period marking the decline of the Spring and Autumn era in ancient China. His aggressive military campaigns dramatically expanded Wu's influence, challenging the traditional hegemonies of Jin and Chu and fundamentally altering the balance of power among the Warring States precursors. Helü's reign is remembered for its strategic brilliance, particularly his employment of Sun Tzu, author of *The Art of War*, and for establishing Wu as a dominant force in the lower Yangtze River region, briefly positioning it as a contender for supremacy over the entire Zhou realm.

Helü's ascension to the throne was preceded by the assassination of his cousin, King Liao of Wu, in 515 BCE, an act orchestrated by Helü with the aid of Zhuan Zhu, a renowned assassin. This bloody coup inaugurated a period of intense military expansion. His most celebrated achievement was the decisive victory over the powerful state of Chu at the Battle of Boju in 506 BCE, a campaign that saw the Wu army, under the command of Sun Tzu and Wu Zixu, march into the Chu capital of Ying. This triumph was followed by the conquest of Yue in 496 BCE, though this victory ultimately led to his fatal wounding by King Goujian of Yue, setting the stage for future conflict.

